format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

lesson atoms-are-everywhere
title: Atoms are everywhere
minutes: 5
xp: 40
teaches: u1-matter-is-atoms
teaches: u1-atom-scale

description:
    Everything you can touch, and the air around you, is made of atoms. You will see what counts as matter and what does not. Then you will feel how small an atom is.

cite ref-openstax-chem-ch1
supports: Matter is anything that occupies space and has mass; gases such as air are matter.

cite ref-openstax-chem-ch2
supports: Atoms are about 10^-10 m across; the nucleus is about 100,000 times smaller.

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Look around you
figure: matter-vs-energy
figure_at: 1

body:
    Look at anything near you, like a cup, a phone or your own hand. All of it is [[matter|matter]], and all matter is built from tiny pieces called [[atom|atoms]]. Light and heat are different: they are energy, and energy is not made of atoms. So for anything you meet, ask one question: is it matter, or is it energy?

card c03
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-x-not-atoms
connected: true

card c12
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Air is matter too
figure: air-balloons
figure_at: 2

body:
    Air feels like nothing because you cannot see it, but it is matter too. You can show it: a pumped-up ball weighs more than a flat one. The only thing that changed is the air inside, so air must be made of atoms that have weight.

card c13
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-l1-tom-air-empty
connected: true

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Far too small to see
figure: zoom-chain
figure_at: 2

body:
    So if everything is made of atoms, why can't we see them? Because they are extremely small. Zoom in on a skin cell and you are still far from an atom. An ordinary microscope uses light, and light is too coarse to show something that small.

